1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 Alright, we're going to spend a few videos talking about the basics of linearization.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 I know this might seem dry or might seem like revision, but I think it's extremely important.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 Linearizing a state-space model, keeping in mind that state-space models are kind of multi-variable vector equations, is a non-trivial task.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 It's actually pretty difficult.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
 So what we're going to do is kind of walk through the different levels of complexity.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
 We're going to start with linearizing scalar functions, and then we're going to move on to linearizing multi-variable functions.

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 Finally, we'll get to linearizing state-space models.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 I'm going to try and draw some analogs between these things so that hopefully you start to see that there's a pattern underneath all of it that linearization, kind of irrespective of the dimension, always kind of works the same way.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:51,000
 And maybe in contrast to your previous studies, I'm not going to do this strictly from the perspective of the Taylor series.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:53,000
 I'm going to try and do it a little bit graphically.

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:57,000
 So in this video, we're going to talk about 1D linearization.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 Say that I have a function, f of x, that looks like this.

13
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 And that I want to build a linearization to that function about some point xa at a approximation point.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 Depending on where xa is in the function, that linearization is going to look pretty different, right?

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 If it's over here, it's quite steep.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
 If it's over here, it's quite shallow.

17
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 But in general, it's a straight line that it's tangent to the function at the point where I'm approximating.

18
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:25,000
 kind of a key thing to note is our approximation.

19
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 Our linear approximation is perfect at the point where we approximate.

20
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
 This is kind of by definition.

21
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 And it gets worse the further away from where we approximated.

22
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 So given some nonlinear function f and an approximation point xa for approximation,

23
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 our goal is to build a linear approximation to this function, which we're going to call f tilde,

24
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 that will allow us to evaluate it for some other x, some query point,

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 maybe I'll call it xq, to work out what f tilde or xq is.

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 And there's a somewhat unsatisfying kind of explanation for how this works.

27
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 If you look at it from the perspective of the Taylor series,

28
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 you can start with the kind of higher order terms and boil it down to a linear term.

29
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 And that'll give you the equation for your linearization.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
 But I've never really been wholly satisfied with how that works.

31
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
 So here's the graphical approach.

32
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 If I want to know f tilde at xq, the value of my approximation at the query point,

33
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 the all I need to do is kind of work out the value of the function at my approximation point and add the difference, right?

34
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 What is that difference?

35
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 Well, our approximation is a straight line, right?

36
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 And remember what we know about straight lines.

37
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:37,000
 If you know its rise and you know its run, you can calculate its gradient.

38
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:39,000
 Or set another way.

39
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 If you know its gradient and you know its run,

40
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:46,000
 the kind of distance in the horizontal direction, you can calculate the rise.

41
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 And that's exactly the approach we take in with building our linearization.

42
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
 The value of our approximation at the query point, f tilde at xq,

43
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 is just the value of the function at our approximation point plus the difference.

44
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
 Plus the gradient of our approximation times the distance from our approximation to the query point.

45
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 What is the gradient of this line?

46
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 Well, by definition, since it's a straight line, the gradient is the derivative.

47
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
 The derivative of the nonlinear function.

48
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
 Let me say that again and be a little bit more general this time.

49
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
 If I have some nonlinear function f,

50
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 and I want to take a linear approximation to that function about the point xa,

51
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 the approximation point,

52
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 then all I need to do is kind of work out the value of the function at xa

53
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 and add to that kind of the gradient of my approximation,

54
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 the derivative of the function at the approximation point,

55
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 multiplied by the distance from my approximation to the point x that I care about.

56
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 These two concepts, the kind of graphical approach,

57
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 but also the fact that a linearization is just kind of a function value plus a gradient times the distance.

58
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 They're going to keep coming up whether we're talking about 1D linearization or multi-variable linearization,

59
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
 or multi-variable vector linearization or linearization of state-space models.

60
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 So we'll see it again in a couple of videos time.

61
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 But before we do, I think it's wise for us to work through a 1D problem.

62
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 Just so we build up a little bit of muscle memory and get some intuition.

63
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:08,000
 Let's have a go at problem 6.1 together.
